  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Foreclosed properties often come with a lot of unknowns, and the subfloor beneath that old carpet or crumbling tile is definitely one of them. You might encounter moisture damage, warped sections, mold, or even some questionable DIY repairs from previous owners. So, what's the game plan? First, you've got to inspect that subfloor thoroughly and check for any moisture issues. You'd be surprised what can be hiding under there! Next, any failed old flooring needs to come up. Then, the crucial step: repair and level that subfloor. This could involve patching, shimming, or even replacing sections to create a solid, even base. After that, if you're using certain materials, you'll need to let your new flooring acclimate to the environment. Then comes the installation, followed by adding the trim and transitions for a polished look. Finally, a thorough inspection and any last touch-ups. It's easy to get caught up in picking out the new flooring, but don't skip the subfloor prep – it's the foundation for a lasting installation. When in doubt, or if you find serious issues like extensive water damage, HH Remodeling LLC can help you navigate these tricky situations."
    }
  ],
